  "image": "public/9840928-ShapingEDU Card Images (22).png",
  "html": "<p>The video below provides an authentic model of the same teacher sorting the texts you saw gathered in the video in the previous chapter. <a href=\"https://youtu.be/EN2VEOwUT7c\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span>Watch on YouTube</span></a> to turn on closed captions/subtitles.</p><div data-type=\"video\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-identifier=\"EN2VEOwUT7c\"></div><h3 id=\"h_considering-student-agency\" class=\"text-2xl font-semibold\">Considering Student Agency</h3><p>As a previous participant in this course, NWP Teacher Leader, Trey Smith, noted questions he thinks through as he considers the opportunities for agency he provides for his students in creating a text set. As you read through the questions, consider opportunities you may provide for your students to have agency in making decisions about the text set you are creating.</p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-6\"><li class=\"\"><p><b>What could it look like to sequence texts so that students’ own questions necessitate the layering on of additional texts?</b> Is it useful to think of text set curation by a teacher to be so spot-on that texts are layered on as just-in-time resources that students feel like they need to move forward?</p></li><li class=\"\"><p><b>How many of the texts are provided by me, the teacher? And how am I making space for students to select and share texts?</b> If I am the primary curator, is our discussion such that students feel like layering on additional texts is warranted? Do they want to see and read more? Have I curated and sequenced texts in a way such that I introduce new layers because I am responding to what students feel they need and want to know next? This feels important for recognizing student agency and for scaffolding research processes.</p></li></ul><p></p><a class=\"self-center\"><img src=\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.appolearning.files/production/uploads/uploaded_file/5710f499-e09c-4679-b0bd-cf608ec89bb6/4.svg\"/></a><p></p><p>To help you sort, you might want to notice:</p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-6\"><li class=\"\"><p>Patterns that emerge from the text types, perspectives, purpose, etc.</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>A range of complexity among the texts.</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>Different types of perspectives that are represented.</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>The variety of text types for students to explore.</p></li></ul><p></p><a class=\"self-center\"><img src=\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.appolearning.files/production/uploads/uploaded_file/8e79b945-8929-4a29-95f8-49b0390573e9/Explore_and_Learn__2_.svg\"/></a><p>In the discussion, reflect on how you chose to sort your texts. After you write your reflection, be sure to read the reflections of others in your group and respond with feedback, questions, or suggestions.</p><a class=\"self-center\"><img src=\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.appolearning.files/production/uploads/uploaded_file/fede44ee-df6f-4146-96d1-a011c5b98b4b/Discussion_icon_2022_copy_small__1_.png\"/></a><p><b>Reflection Prompts:</b></p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-6\"><li class=\"\"><p>What categories did you come up with for sorting your texts?</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>What patterns emerged?</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>Were there any texts you weren&#39;t sure how to sort? If so, why?</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>What questions do you have about your text set at this point (this is where your discussion group can really help you out!)?</p></li></ul>",
